;;; `std.bijection`'s activators — the fan-out **with the blind enumerator
;;; left on**, which is what every other program in this directory turns off.
;;;
;;; ── Why this file exists ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The blind enumerator builds candidates from every relation carrying a
;;; `(relation …)` signature, over the program's names, so the question "could
;;; a run start guessing about the library's rules themselves" is a real one,
;;; and the answer must not be an argument about names. It is this file: the
;;; four obligation rules of the library are in the program, `total-owed` and
;;; `surjective-owed` from `std.algebra`, `slot-owed-room` and `slot-owed-fill`
;;; from `std.slots`, each imported whole, the enumerator runs unscoped, and
;;; `ein-infer/tests/obligation_reports.rs` asserts that no `hyp` event it
;;; emits names any of them, as a fact's relation or as an argument.
;;;
;;; The enumerator runs only where root holds no `open` fact and no obligation
;;; is activated (Q-M1f.576), so the file activates none: `bijective-setup`'s
;;; activators are written by hand, every one but `(total-owed seats is-a)` and
;;; `(surjective-owed seats is-a)`, which would activate an obligation. The six
;;; activators that are there — `total`, `surjective`, `domain-elimination`,
;;; `range-elimination`, `functional-negative`, `injective-negative` — are
;;; covered by the same check.
;;;
;;; ── The puzzle ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Two guests, two chairs, one seating stated. The bijection machinery closes
;;; it without a single hypothesis: `functional-negative` and
;;; `injective-negative` write the two negatives Ann's chair implies, and
;;; `domain-elimination` then forces Bob into the chair that is left. So the
;;; verdict is a `Solution` at root — and the enumerator still ran, because the
;;; universe is what asks it whether anything is left to guess.
;;;
;;; ── What `:no-hypothesis` names, and what it deliberately does not ─────────
;;;
;;; The two hierarchy relations and `sort`, and nothing else. A type hierarchy
;;; is not a thing to guess about — left in, the enumerator proposes
;;; `(is-a Ann Bob)` and every relative of it, and the search grows past
;;; anything a test should pay; `sort` is the same kind of relation and is
;;; scoped out for the same reason, which also keeps this file's counters
;;; small. Everything else stays in scope, and that is the point: **the
;;; library's rules are not named here**, so if the enumerator could propose
;;; a fact about one it would, and the assertion would catch it.
;;;
;;; The typecheck's activators are written for the same reason: with the
;;; enumerator loose on `seats`, the kernel imposes no type system (Q23) and
;;; nothing else would refuse `(seats C1 Ann)` — a chair sitting on a guest.
;;; The puzzle's own typecheck is what rejects it.
;;;
;;; ── What the expectation observes ──────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Naming `seats` closes it, so the claim is that the extension is exactly the
;;; two arrows and the two negatives — which is also the claim that
;;; `domain-elimination` fired rather than a guess landing.

(import std.algebra)
(import std.bijection)
(import std.slots)

(relation is-a  T T           :why "{?3} is a {?4}")
(relation is-a* T T           :why "{?3} is a {?4} (transitively)")
(relation seats Guest Chair   :why "{?3} sits on {?4}")
(relation sort  T T           :why "{?3} is a declared sort of {?4}")

(is-a Ann Guest) (is-a Bob Guest)
(is-a C1 Chair)  (is-a C2 Chair)

(sort Guest Sortal) (sort Chair Sortal)

;; The typecheck quantifies over the transitive hierarchy; there is no
;; inheritance here, so it is `is-a` copied across.
(std.algebra.includes is-a is-a*)

;; `bijective-setup`'s activators, but the two obligations'.
(std.algebra.functional seats) (std.algebra.injective seats)
(std.algebra.total seats) (std.algebra.surjective seats)
(std.algebra.total seats is-a) (std.algebra.surjective seats is-a)
(std.bijection.domain-elimination seats is-a) (std.bijection.range-elimination seats is-a)
(std.bijection.functional-negative seats is-a) (std.bijection.injective-negative seats is-a)
;; `typecheck-setup`'s.
(std.bijection.typecheck-arg-0 seats is-a* Guest) (std.bijection.typecheck-arg-1 seats is-a* Chair)

(seats Ann C1)

(solve
  :no-hypothesis (is-a is-a* sort)
  :expect (region (model (seats Ann C1) (seats Bob C2)
                         (not (seats Ann C2)) (not (seats Bob C1)))))

(query
  :goal (seats ?who ?where))
